{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000001","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Strategy","concept":"Strategy Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should interchangeable algorithms or policies be represented without embedding their selection logic throughout the caller?","answer":"Represent each interchangeable behavior behind a stable callable or interface and select the behavior at a composition boundary.","explanation":"The Strategy pattern separates a varying algorithm or policy from the code that invokes it. The caller depends on the behavior contract rather than the concrete algorithm.","principle":"Encapsulate what varies; keep the consumer independent of concrete strategy implementations.","rule":"Selection of a strategy should occur at a boundary such as configuration, construction, dependency composition, or explicit policy selection.","correct_example":"A pricing component accepts a strategy such as StandardPricing, DiscountPricing, or PremiumPricing through a common callable/interface.","invalid_example":"A single large function repeatedly branches on customer_type and embeds every pricing algorithm directly.","problem":"Conditional logic grows as new algorithms are added.","failure_mode":"The strategy abstraction becomes unnecessary indirection when only one stable algorithm exists.","cause":"Variation exists but has not been isolated, or abstraction was introduced without real variation.","solution":"Extract the varying behavior behind a narrow contract and keep orchestration outside the strategies.","alternative":"A direct conditional or pattern match can be preferable when the set of cases is closed and small.","tradeoff":"Improves extensibility and testing isolation but adds an abstraction and potentially more indirection.","constraint":"Use only when behavior genuinely varies independently of the consumer.","edge_case":"A strategy may require configuration unavailable to the caller; inject that configuration explicitly rather than using hidden global state.","security_implication":"Policy selection must not allow untrusted input to select privileged behavior without authorization checks.","performance_implication":"Dispatch overhead is usually small but can matter in extremely hot paths; measure before replacing a direct call.","testing_guidance":"Test each strategy independently and test the consumer against representative strategy implementations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that adding a new strategy does not require modification of the consumer's core algorithm.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-version dependent; pattern itself is language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000002","D06-PAT-000011"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Official C++ guidance supports explicit, strongly typed interfaces and precise contracts."},{"source_ref":"turn0search3","relevance":"Official Rust documentation demonstrates functions and closures used as interchangeable behavior."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000002","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Strategy","concept":"Function-Based Strategy","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can a Strategy be represented by a function rather than a class hierarchy?","answer":"When the varying behavior can be expressed as a callable with a sufficiently stable input/output contract, a function or closure can serve as the strategy.","explanation":"Many languages treat functions as values, allowing behavior to be passed directly without creating a separate class for every strategy.","principle":"Prefer the smallest abstraction that expresses the required variation.","rule":"Use a callable strategy when the behavior does not require substantial persistent object state or a large polymorphic interface.","correct_example":"calculate_total(items, pricing_policy) where pricing_policy accepts an item collection and returns a total.","invalid_example":"Creating multiple one-method classes solely to wrap trivial stateless functions when the language already supports first-class callables.","problem":"The implementation becomes more verbose than the behavior warrants.","failure_mode":"A function-based strategy becomes unclear when the behavior needs multiple related operations or substantial state.","cause":"The abstraction boundary no longer matches the complexity of the behavior.","solution":"Promote the strategy to a richer interface/object only when the contract genuinely requires it.","alternative":"Interface/trait/class-based Strategy.","tradeoff":"Lower ceremony and direct composition versus less explicit structure for complex strategies.","constraint":"The callable contract must be explicit enough for callers and tooling to reason about it.","security_implication":"A callable supplied dynamically should be treated as executable behavior and must originate from a trusted composition boundary.","performance_implication":"Function indirection may be measurable in tight loops; benchmark only where profiling identifies it as relevant.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic strategy functions and test the caller with multiple strategy implementations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that strategy substitution preserves the consumer's required behavioral contract.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Rust; Go","version_range":"Depends on language support for first-class functions/closures.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000001","D06-PAT-000003"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search3","relevance":"Rust official documentation describes closures and functions as values passed to algorithms."},{"source_ref":"turn0search14","relevance":"Rust official documentation describes functional-style use of functions and closures."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000003","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Creational Patterns","subtopic":"Factory","concept":"Factory Method","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should object creation be delegated to a factory method?","answer":"Delegate creation when callers should depend on an abstraction while construction varies by subtype, configuration, environment, or policy.","explanation":"A factory method localizes construction decisions so callers do not need to know concrete implementation details.","principle":"Separate object-use decisions from object-construction decisions when construction is itself variable.","rule":"Keep construction logic in one explicit boundary when multiple callers would otherwise duplicate or depend on concrete constructors.","correct_example":"A parser factory returns a parser implementation selected from a validated format identifier.","invalid_example":"A factory containing dozens of unrelated object types and business rules that effectively becomes a hidden service locator.","problem":"Concrete construction leaks into many callers or becomes duplicated.","failure_mode":"Factory complexity grows until the factory becomes harder to maintain than direct construction.","cause":"Too many unrelated responsibilities were centralized.","solution":"Split factories by cohesive creation responsibility and keep selection rules explicit.","alternative":"Direct constructors for simple stable objects; dependency injection for externally supplied implementations.","tradeoff":"Centralizes creation and reduces coupling but adds another abstraction layer.","constraint":"The factory must expose a clear creation contract and should not conceal unrelated side effects.","edge_case":"Unknown creation keys must produce a controlled failure rather than silently returning an unintended implementation.","security_implication":"Never allow arbitrary user-controlled class/module names to become dynamic construction instructions.","performance_implication":"Creation overhead is normally dominated by the created object's initialization; avoid caching solely because a factory exists.","testing_guidance":"Test every supported construction path and unsupported input paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that returned objects satisfy the advertised abstraction contract.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent pattern.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000004","D06-PAT-000006"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Official C++ guidance emphasizes explicit, strongly typed interfaces and contracts."},{"source_ref":"turn0search7","relevance":"Oracle documentation demonstrates controlled type hierarchies through sealed interfaces/classes."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000004","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Creational Patterns","subtopic":"Factory","concept":"Factory as Policy Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a factory prevent construction rules from spreading across application code?","answer":"Treat the factory as a narrow policy boundary that maps validated requirements to permitted implementations.","explanation":"The useful role of a factory is not merely hiding constructors; it can centralize a bounded construction policy while keeping consumers independent of concrete types.","principle":"Centralize decisions that must remain consistent across consumers.","rule":"A factory should perform creation-related policy only and should not become a general-purpose application service.","correct_example":"create_storage(kind, config) selects one of explicitly supported storage implementations after validating kind and required configuration.","invalid_example":"create_everything(name, user_input, database, network, logging, authorization) where one factory becomes responsible for unrelated orchestration.","problem":"Construction rules become inconsistent between callers.","failure_mode":"Factory becomes a god object.","cause":"Too many unrelated decisions were moved into one creation function.","solution":"Split the creation boundary according to cohesive responsibilities.","alternative":"Composition root with dependency injection.","tradeoff":"Consistency improves but the factory becomes another dependency that requires maintenance.","constraint":"Supported implementations should be explicit and bounded.","edge_case":"A valid implementation may be unavailable in the current environment; fail explicitly and preserve diagnostic context.","security_implication":"Factory input must not bypass authorization or instantiate privileged components merely because a type is technically available.","performance_implication":"Avoid repeated expensive discovery during construction; perform explicit caching only when its lifetime semantics are understood.","testing_guidance":"Test each supported mapping and each invalid configuration path.","verification_guidance":"Verify implementation selection against the declared policy matrix.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000003","D06-PAT-000014"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Official C++ guidance emphasizes precise interfaces and explicit contracts."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000005","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Creational Patterns","subtopic":"Factory","concept":"Closed-Set Factory","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is a closed set of implementations preferable to arbitrary extensibility?","answer":"A closed set is preferable when the valid implementations are intentionally constrained and exhaustive reasoning is valuable.","explanation":"Explicitly enumerated implementations can make unsupported cases visible and allow stronger validation than arbitrary plugin discovery.","principle":"Do not introduce open-ended extensibility when the domain intentionally has a bounded set of variants.","rule":"Use a closed mapping when correctness depends on knowing all permitted variants.","correct_example":"A command parser accepts only explicitly supported command variants and rejects unknown variants.","invalid_example":"Automatically importing any module whose name appears in external configuration.","problem":"The implementation set is expected to be controlled but the design permits arbitrary extensions.","failure_mode":"Unexpected implementations enter the system without the assumptions being reviewed.","cause":"Extensibility was prioritized over bounded correctness.","solution":"Use an explicit registry or closed mapping and require deliberate registration for extensions.","alternative":"Plugin architecture when third-party extension is an actual requirement.","tradeoff":"Closed designs improve predictability; open designs improve extensibility.","constraint":"Closed-set assumptions must be documented and tested.","edge_case":"Future versions may add a valid variant; version-aware handling must distinguish unknown from newly introduced values.","security_implication":"Closed sets reduce the attack surface created by arbitrary code loading.","performance_implication":"Explicit dispatch can be simpler and cheaper than dynamic discovery.","testing_guidance":"Test every allowed variant plus unknown and malformed variants.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no unregistered implementation can enter through the factory boundary.","language":"Java; Rust; TypeScript; C++; Go; Python","version_range":"Language-dependent implementation.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000003","D06-PAT-000041"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search7","relevance":"Oracle documents sealed hierarchies that explicitly restrict permitted subclasses."},{"source_ref":"turn0search0","relevance":"Rust documentation demonstrates enums as explicit sets of variants."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000006","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Creational Patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Builder Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should construction of a complex object be separated from the final object representation?","answer":"Use a Builder when construction has many optional, ordered, conditional, or semantically distinct configuration steps that would otherwise create an unclear constructor or invalid intermediate state.","explanation":"The Builder pattern makes complex construction explicit and can provide a controlled point at which the final object is assembled.","principle":"Separate complex construction from the representation being constructed.","rule":"A Builder should make valid construction easier and invalid construction harder, not merely wrap a trivial constructor.","correct_example":"A request builder collects optional headers, timeout policy, authentication mode, and payload before producing a validated request object.","invalid_example":"A Builder containing only one setter and a final constructor with no meaningful construction complexity.","problem":"Large constructors become difficult to read and easy to misuse.","failure_mode":"Builder becomes ceremony without reducing construction complexity.","cause":"The object was not complex enough to justify the pattern.","solution":"Use a direct constructor, named constructor, factory, or language-specific initialization feature when simpler.","alternative":"Named arguments, records/data classes, factory functions, or parameter objects.","tradeoff":"Improves construction readability and validation opportunities but adds lifecycle and API surface.","constraint":"The final build operation should define the point where required invariants are checked.","edge_case":"A builder reused after build may accidentally carry state into a new object; define reuse semantics explicitly.","security_implication":"Sensitive configuration should not be silently retained in reusable builders or diagnostic representations.","performance_implication":"Builder allocations and intermediate objects may matter in high-volume construction; measure before optimizing.","testing_guidance":"Test minimal valid construction, maximal construction, invalid combinations, missing required values, and repeated build behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify final objects satisfy required invariants regardless of construction order where order is not intended to matter.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent; implementation differs by language idioms.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000007","D06-PAT-000008"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search8","relevance":"Python classes provide object construction and behavior grouping; Builder may be appropriate only when construction complexity warrants it."},{"source_ref":"turn0search6","relevance":"Oracle documents record classes as concise data carriers, providing an alternative to verbose construction patterns."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000007","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Creational Patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Fluent Builder","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is fluent configuration useful in a Builder?","answer":"Fluent configuration is useful when chained operations correspond to a coherent construction vocabulary and the resulting call sequence remains readable.","explanation":"A fluent API can make configuration resemble a declarative construction sequence, but excessive chaining can obscure control flow and validation.","principle":"Optimize the construction API for semantic clarity, not chaining length.","rule":"Each fluent operation should represent a meaningful construction decision and preserve an understandable contract.","correct_example":"RequestBuilder().method(\"GET\").timeout(2).header(\"Accept\",\"application/json\").build().","invalid_example":"Builder chains containing dozens of low-level mutations whose ordering is undocumented.","problem":"Complex configuration becomes difficult to review.","failure_mode":"A fluent interface becomes a hidden mutable state machine with surprising ordering requirements.","cause":"Too many operations or implicit dependencies between operations.","solution":"Group related configuration, enforce invariants at build time, and make ordering constraints explicit where unavoidable.","alternative":"Immutable configuration object with named parameters.","tradeoff":"Readable for moderate configuration but can become verbose or order-sensitive at scale.","constraint":"The fluent API must document which operations are optional, required, mutually exclusive, or order-sensitive.","edge_case":"Calling build twice may either produce equivalent independent objects or be forbidden; define the contract.","security_implication":"Do not let fluent configuration bypass mandatory security settings through defaults that are silently insecure.","performance_implication":"Chaining itself is rarely the bottleneck; construction and validation costs should be measured separately.","testing_guidance":"Test operation ordering, mutually exclusive options, defaults, and repeated build calls.","verification_guidance":"Verify the final configuration rather than merely verifying that every builder method was invoked.","language":"Java; TypeScript; C++; Python; Rust","version_range":"Language-dependent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000006"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ Core Guidelines emphasize explicit interfaces and pre/postconditions."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000008","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Creational Patterns","subtopic":"Builder","concept":"Validated Construction Boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Where should object invariants be checked when using a Builder?","answer":"Required invariants should be checked at the final construction boundary and, where useful, earlier checks may provide better diagnostics.","explanation":"A Builder should not allow a caller to mistake a partially configured object for a valid completed object.","principle":"Establish validity at the point where the object becomes externally usable.","rule":"Final construction must not succeed when mandatory invariants are violated.","correct_example":"build() rejects a configuration missing a required endpoint or containing mutually incompatible options.","invalid_example":"build() always returns an object while assuming callers remembered every required field.","problem":"Invalid state escapes the construction boundary.","failure_mode":"Failures occur much later and become harder to diagnose.","cause":"Validation was distributed inconsistently or omitted.","solution":"Define construction invariants explicitly and enforce them at finalization.","alternative":"Type-level construction states where the language can encode them economically.","tradeoff":"Runtime validation is broadly applicable; type-state designs can provide stronger compile-time guarantees but increase complexity.","constraint":"Do not claim compile-time enforcement where the language/type system cannot actually provide it.","edge_case":"Validation dependent on external state should be separated from purely structural validation when possible.","security_implication":"Security-critical invariants must be enforced independently of caller intent.","performance_implication":"Repeated validation can add cost; distinguish cheap local invariants from expensive external checks.","testing_guidance":"Create tests for every invariant and combinations of invalid configuration.","verification_guidance":"Verify successful construction implies the documented invariant set, not merely object existence.","language":"Python; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-dependent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000006","D06-PAT-000025"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Official C++ guidance explicitly addresses preconditions and postconditions."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000009","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Structural Patterns","subtopic":"Adapter","concept":"Adapter Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can an incompatible existing interface be used without rewriting the existing implementation?","answer":"Wrap the existing implementation with an Adapter that translates the consumer's expected contract into the adaptee's contract.","explanation":"Adapter isolates interface incompatibility at a boundary rather than forcing unrelated consumers or implementations to change.","principle":"Translate contracts at the boundary where incompatibility occurs.","rule":"The Adapter should translate semantics faithfully and should not silently change important behavior.","correct_example":"A PaymentGatewayAdapter converts the application's authorize(request) contract into a legacy client's create_transaction call.","invalid_example":"Adding compatibility conditionals to every consumer of the legacy API.","problem":"Consumers become coupled to legacy interface details.","failure_mode":"Adapter hides incompatible semantics such as different failure, timeout, or data interpretation rules.","cause":"Only syntax was adapted while semantic differences were ignored.","solution":"Document and test semantic translation, including error and edge-case behavior.","alternative":"Facade when the goal is to simplify a compatible subsystem rather than translate incompatibility.","tradeoff":"Localizes compatibility cost but adds a translation layer.","constraint":"The adapter contract must explicitly document behavior that cannot be translated exactly.","edge_case":"Legacy API may return ambiguous values; adapter must preserve or explicitly resolve ambiguity rather than inventing meaning.","security_implication":"Input/output translation must not weaken authorization, validation, encoding, or trust boundaries.","performance_implication":"Translation can add allocations, copying, serialization, or conversion costs.","testing_guidance":"Test contract equivalence, error translation, boundary values, and unsupported operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify representative calls against both contracts and confirm semantic equivalence where promised.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000010","D06-PAT-000015"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ guidance emphasizes explicit interfaces and contracts, relevant to adapter boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000010","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Structural Patterns","subtopic":"Adapter","concept":"Anti-Corruption Adapter Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should a system prevent an external or legacy model from leaking into its internal model?","answer":"Place translation at a dedicated boundary and keep the internal domain model independent of the external representation.","explanation":"An adapter can serve as an anti-corruption boundary when an external representation has concepts, naming, error behavior, or lifecycle rules that should not become internal design assumptions.","principle":"External representation is not automatically internal domain truth.","rule":"Translate external concepts into an explicitly defined internal contract rather than exposing external types throughout the system.","correct_example":"An external customer DTO is converted into an internal Customer value model at the integration boundary.","invalid_example":"Passing vendor-specific DTOs through business logic, storage, and tests until the entire system depends on the vendor model.","problem":"External coupling spreads across the codebase.","failure_mode":"Replacing the external system becomes a cross-cutting rewrite.","cause":"The integration boundary was not enforced.","solution":"Centralize translation and keep internal interfaces independent.","alternative":"Direct integration when the external model is intentionally the application's canonical model.","tradeoff":"Adds translation and maintenance cost but protects internal architecture from external change.","constraint":"Do not create needless duplicate models when the external model is already an intentional domain contract.","edge_case":"Lossy translation must be explicit; information that cannot be represented internally should not disappear silently.","security_implication":"Translation is an opportunity to validate and minimize untrusted external data before it reaches trusted components.","performance_implication":"Conversion cost should be measured where high-volume data crosses the boundary.","testing_guidance":"Use contract tests against external representations and unit tests for translation rules.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all required semantic information survives translation and that forbidden external details do not leak inward.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000009","D06-PAT-000026"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Explicit interface guidance supports stable boundaries between components."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000011","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Decorator","concept":"Decorator Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can behavior be added around an existing component without changing its core implementation?","answer":"Wrap the component with another object or callable that preserves the required interface while adding a focused behavior before, after, or around delegation.","explanation":"Decorator composes behavior around a component without requiring inheritance or modification of the wrapped implementation.","principle":"Prefer composable behavior when extensions can be expressed as wrappers around a stable contract.","rule":"A decorator should preserve the underlying contract unless the changed behavior is explicitly part of the new contract.","correct_example":"A service decorator adds metrics around calls to an underlying service while preserving the service interface.","invalid_example":"A decorator that silently changes return semantics, error meaning, or authorization behavior while claiming interface equivalence.","problem":"Cross-cutting behavior is duplicated across many implementations.","failure_mode":"Nested decorators create unclear ordering and unexpected interactions.","cause":"Too many independent wrappers or implicit ordering dependencies.","solution":"Keep decorators narrow, document ordering-sensitive behavior, and test compositions.","alternative":"Middleware pipeline, explicit orchestration, inheritance, or direct implementation.","tradeoff":"Excellent composability but potentially harder tracing/debugging when many decorators are stacked.","constraint":"Each decorator should have one coherent responsibility.","edge_case":"Decorator ordering can change semantics; treat order as part of the composition contract when material.","security_implication":"Security decorators must fail closed and must not be bypassable through alternate paths to the wrapped component.","performance_implication":"Each layer can add call overhead, logging, allocation, or I/O; measure stacked paths.","testing_guidance":"Test the decorator independently and test important decorator combinations.","verification_guidance":"Verify preservation of the underlying contract plus the decorator's additional guarantee.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000001","D06-PAT-000012"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search8","relevance":"Python classes support composition and method overriding needed for decorator-style objects."},{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ interface guidance supports explicit contracts between wrapped components."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000012","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Decorator","concept":"Composable Cross-Cutting Decorator","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is a decorator suitable for cross-cutting concerns?","answer":"A decorator is suitable when a concern can be applied uniformly around a stable interface without requiring the wrapped implementation to know about that concern.","explanation":"Logging, metrics, caching, authorization checks, retries, and tracing can sometimes be modeled as wrappers, but the semantics of each concern must be considered before composition.","principle":"Cross-cutting behavior should be composed at a boundary where its scope is explicit.","rule":"Do not use decorators to conceal complex business workflows or materially alter the meaning of the wrapped operation.","correct_example":"A repository decorator records operation duration while delegating the actual persistence operation.","invalid_example":"A decorator silently retries non-idempotent business operations without exposing retry semantics.","problem":"Cross-cutting behavior becomes duplicated or entangled with business logic.","failure_mode":"The wrapper changes semantics unexpectedly.","cause":"A concern that is not behavior-preserving was treated as a transparent decorator.","solution":"Define the semantic contract and expose behavior-changing wrappers explicitly.","alternative":"Middleware or explicit orchestration.","tradeoff":"Strong modularity for truly cross-cutting concerns versus hidden control flow if overused.","constraint":"The scope and ordering of the decorator must be visible to maintainers.","edge_case":"Nested caching, retry, and authorization decorators may produce surprising interactions.","security_implication":"Authorization and security wrappers must be placed where every relevant execution path passes through them.","performance_implication":"Metrics and logging can introduce latency and I/O; sampling or asynchronous processing may be appropriate depending on system requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test both isolated decorators and critical compositions, including failure paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the decorated operation satisfies both the original contract and the added concern's guarantee.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000011","D06-PAT-000020"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ Core Guidelines emphasize explicit interfaces and contracts, which are prerequisites for safe wrapping."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000013","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Command","concept":"Command Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can an operation be represented as a first-class value that can be queued, logged, retried, or tested?","answer":"Represent the operation and its required parameters as a command object or callable with an explicit execution contract.","explanation":"Command separates the representation of an action from the component that invokes it, enabling delayed or controlled execution.","principle":"Represent an operation explicitly when its lifecycle matters independently of immediate execution.","rule":"A command should define what execution means, including failure and repeatability semantics where relevant.","correct_example":"A SendEmailCommand stores validated message data and exposes execute() through an authorized executor.","invalid_example":"A command object that directly accesses global application state and performs unrelated operations during construction.","problem":"Operations need queuing, auditing, composition, or controlled execution.","failure_mode":"Command objects become data bags containing arbitrary hidden behavior.","cause":"No clear command contract or boundary.","solution":"Keep command state explicit and execution responsibility bounded.","alternative":"Direct function call for immediate simple operations.","tradeoff":"Improves explicitness and orchestration at the cost of additional objects or serialization complexity.","constraint":"Serializable commands must define how code version and data compatibility are maintained.","edge_case":"A command may execute twice due to retries; idempotency must be designed where duplicate execution is possible.","security_implication":"A command is executable authority; validate who may construct, submit, and execute it separately where needed.","performance_implication":"Command allocation and serialization can add overhead in high-volume systems.","testing_guidance":"Test command validation, execution, failure, and repeated invocation semantics.","verification_guidance":"Verify that execution produces the documented state transition and that unauthorized commands are rejected.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000014","D06-PAT-000023"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search3","relevance":"Rust documentation supports treating functions and closures as values, which can implement lightweight commands."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000014","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Command","concept":"Idempotent Command Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should commands behave when execution may be retried?","answer":"Commands whose execution can be repeated should either be intrinsically idempotent or carry an explicit deduplication/idempotency mechanism appropriate to the operation.","explanation":"A command abstraction does not make an operation safe to repeat. Repeatability is a property of the underlying operation and its state transition.","principle":"Separate command representation from idempotency guarantees.","rule":"Never infer idempotency merely because an operation is represented as a command.","correct_example":"A payment command uses a stable operation identifier so repeated submissions resolve to the same logical operation rather than charging twice.","invalid_example":"Blindly retrying an arbitrary create operation because it is wrapped in a Command class.","problem":"Retries may cause duplicate side effects.","failure_mode":"Repeated execution produces multiple externally visible effects.","cause":"No idempotency contract or deduplication mechanism.","solution":"Define idempotency semantics explicitly and implement them at the correct state boundary.","alternative":"At-most-once processing where the system can tolerate lost operations and the semantics justify it.","tradeoff":"Idempotency improves recovery safety but requires state, identifiers, and retention policies.","constraint":"Idempotency keys require a defined scope, uniqueness guarantee, and lifecycle.","edge_case":"A retry arrives after the original operation succeeded but before its result was observed; the system must return or reconstruct the prior outcome when promised.","security_implication":"Idempotency identifiers must not become authorization bypass tokens or expose another user's operation results.","performance_implication":"Deduplication storage and lookup add overhead that scales with retention requirements.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate submissions, concurrent duplicate submissions, delayed retries, and retry-after-failure behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that repeated execution satisfies the documented side-effect invariant.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000013"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Explicit interface and contract guidance supports defining command behavior precisely."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000015","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"State","concept":"State Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can state-dependent behavior be localized instead of spreading conditionals throughout a component?","answer":"Represent state-specific behavior behind state-specific implementations or a controlled state model and delegate behavior according to the current state.","explanation":"The State pattern localizes behavior associated with distinct states so the main object does not accumulate large conditional branches.","principle":"Keep behavior that varies by state close to the state representation.","rule":"Use State when state transitions and state-specific behavior are substantial enough that centralized branching harms maintainability.","correct_example":"A document object delegates publish, edit, and archive behavior to explicit Draft, Review, and Published state implementations.","invalid_example":"Hundreds of scattered boolean checks such as is_draft, is_reviewed, and is_archived across unrelated methods.","problem":"State-dependent behavior becomes difficult to reason about and extend.","failure_mode":"State objects themselves become a hidden collection of inconsistent transitions.","cause":"Transitions were not defined as part of the state model.","solution":"Define valid states, transitions, and state-specific operations explicitly.","alternative":"Enum plus centralized match/switch when the state set is small and behavior is naturally centralized.","tradeoff":"State objects reduce conditional complexity but add types and indirection.","constraint":"State transitions must remain explicit enough to audit.","edge_case":"An invalid transition must fail predictably rather than silently changing state.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive state transitions must enforce authorization and invariants independently of presentation-layer state.","performance_implication":"Dispatch adds overhead but is normally insignificant compared with the cost of the business operation.","testing_guidance":"Test every valid transition, invalid transition, and state-specific behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every reachable state has defined behavior and that illegal transitions cannot occur through supported APIs.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent; Rust also supports state-pattern implementations using traits or enums.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000016","D06-PAT-000017"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search10","relevance":"Rust official documentation explicitly demonstrates an object-oriented State pattern and contrasts it with enum-based state modeling."},{"source_ref":"turn0search7","relevance":"Java sealed hierarchies can constrain state-like subtype sets."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000016","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"State","concept":"Enum-Based State Model","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is an enum or tagged union preferable to polymorphic State objects?","answer":"An enum or tagged union is often preferable when the state set is closed, transitions are naturally centralized, and exhaustive handling provides value.","explanation":"Closed variants can make all possible states explicit and allow the language or compiler to assist with exhaustive handling.","principle":"Use closed data modeling when the domain itself is closed and exhaustive reasoning is valuable.","rule":"Prefer an enum/tagged union when state-specific behavior is simple enough to remain understandable in centralized matching.","correct_example":"A Rust enum represents Draft, Review, and Published states and a match handles state-specific transitions.","invalid_example":"Using dozens of tiny state classes when every state differs only by a small data value and a single exhaustive dispatch would be clearer.","problem":"Polymorphic state implementations create unnecessary structural complexity.","failure_mode":"State abstraction obscures a small, closed state machine.","cause":"Pattern selection was driven by familiarity rather than domain structure.","solution":"Compare closed-data and polymorphic representations before choosing the pattern.","alternative":"State pattern with traits/interfaces/classes.","tradeoff":"Enums improve exhaustiveness and locality; polymorphism can isolate complex state-specific behavior.","constraint":"The enum approach becomes less attractive when state behavior is large, independently extensible, or requires separate implementation ownership.","edge_case":"Adding a new state may require updating every match site; this can be beneficial because omissions become visible.","security_implication":"Closed state representations can reduce unexpected state injection but do not replace authorization controls.","performance_implication":"Centralized dispatch may have simpler runtime behavior than dynamic polymorphism, but actual performance depends on language/compiler/runtime.","testing_guidance":"Test exhaustive state behavior and invalid transitions.","verification_guidance":"Use compiler exhaustiveness checks where available and supplement them with transition tests.","language":"Rust; Java; TypeScript; C++; C; Go","version_range":"Rust enum model is stable; Java sealed classes/interfaces are available in Java 17+; other languages have different mechanisms.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000015","D06-PAT-000018"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search0","relevance":"Rust official documentation describes enums as explicit variants and match-based handling."},{"source_ref":"turn0search7","relevance":"Oracle documents Java sealed classes as restricted extension hierarchies."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000017","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"State","concept":"Explicit Transition Model","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should a stateful component prevent illegal transitions?","answer":"Represent valid transitions explicitly and reject transitions that are not part of the state machine contract.","explanation":"State behavior is incomplete without transition rules. The transition model should be explicit enough for testing, verification, and future maintenance.","principle":"Model state transitions as part of the domain contract rather than as incidental conditionals.","rule":"Every externally reachable transition must either be defined as valid or fail deterministically.","correct_example":"Draft can transition to Review, Review can transition to Published or Draft, and Published cannot transition directly to Draft unless explicitly supported.","invalid_example":"A generic set_state(value) method that allows arbitrary transitions regardless of domain rules.","problem":"Invalid states can be reached through apparently valid APIs.","failure_mode":"Downstream code receives combinations that were assumed impossible.","cause":"State mutation was exposed without transition constraints.","solution":"Replace unrestricted state mutation with explicit transition operations or validated transition functions.","alternative":"Immutable state plus pure transition function.","tradeoff":"Explicit transitions improve correctness but can increase API surface.","constraint":"Transition rules must be kept synchronized with requirements and tests.","edge_case":"Concurrent transition attempts require a separate concurrency correctness strategy; the pattern itself does not solve synchronization.","security_implication":"Authorization must be evaluated for transitions whose permissions differ by current state.","performance_implication":"Transition validation is usually inexpensive; external validation may dominate cost.","testing_guidance":"Generate a transition matrix and test every valid and invalid pair.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the reachable-state graph matches the documented transition graph.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000015","D06-PAT-000016"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search10","relevance":"Rust official State-pattern discussion emphasizes encapsulating state-dependent behavior and evaluating alternative state representations."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000018","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Structural Patterns","subtopic":"Type Modeling","concept":"Closed Variant Modeling","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should a domain concept be modeled as a closed set of variants instead of an open inheritance hierarchy?","answer":"Use closed variants when the complete set of meaningful cases is controlled by the application or specification and exhaustive reasoning is valuable.","explanation":"Closed variant modeling makes the permitted alternatives explicit and can enable exhaustive dispatch.","principle":"Model domain openness or closedness explicitly rather than assuming all hierarchies should be extensible.","rule":"Choose a closed representation when arbitrary third-party extension would violate domain assumptions.","correct_example":"A compiler IR defines a controlled set of expression variants and processes them exhaustively.","invalid_example":"An open plugin hierarchy for a domain where introducing unknown variants would invalidate security or correctness assumptions.","problem":"The design needs exhaustive reasoning over known variants.","failure_mode":"Unexpected subtype enters a supposedly closed domain.","cause":"Open inheritance was used without considering domain closure.","solution":"Use an enum, sealed hierarchy, discriminated union, or equivalent controlled mechanism.","alternative":"Open interface/trait hierarchy when external extension is a real requirement.","tradeoff":"Closed modeling improves exhaustiveness and predictability; open modeling improves extensibility.","constraint":"The choice must reflect actual extension requirements, not theoretical possibilities.","edge_case":"A future version may add variants; version-aware parsing and compatibility rules are required.","security_implication":"Closed variant sets can reduce unexpected code paths and extension attack surface.","performance_implication":"Some languages can optimize closed dispatch; do not assume a performance advantage without measurement.","testing_guidance":"Test all variants and unknown/future-version representations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unsupported variants cannot silently enter the system as valid known variants.","language":"Rust; Java; TypeScript; C++; C; Go","version_range":"Mechanism differs by language. Java sealed classes are permanent from Java 17; Rust enums are core language constructs.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000005","D06-PAT-000016"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search7","relevance":"Oracle documents sealed classes/interfaces as restricted hierarchies."},{"source_ref":"turn0search0","relevance":"Rust documentation describes enums as explicit variant sets."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000019","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Observer","concept":"Observer Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can multiple consumers react to a change without the producer knowing their concrete implementations?","answer":"Expose a subscription contract and notify registered observers when the relevant event or state change occurs.","explanation":"Observer separates event producers from consumers and can reduce direct coupling when many consumers need to react to the same event.","principle":"Depend on an event contract rather than concrete consumers.","rule":"Observer implementations must define subscription lifecycle, notification semantics, failure behavior, and ordering expectations.","correct_example":"A domain event publisher notifies registered read-only projections after a validated state change.","invalid_example":"A global observer registry where arbitrary components subscribe forever and receive every event in the application.","problem":"Direct notification creates coupling between producer and many consumers.","failure_mode":"Observers leak subscriptions or make producer execution depend on slow/failing consumers.","cause":"Lifecycle and delivery semantics were not explicitly modeled.","solution":"Define bounded subscriptions, notification contracts, failure isolation, and lifecycle ownership.","alternative":"Explicit event bus or message broker when stronger delivery guarantees are required.","tradeoff":"Loose coupling improves extensibility but increases reasoning complexity around ordering and delivery.","constraint":"Observer is not automatically durable, asynchronous, ordered, or exactly-once.","edge_case":"Observer removal during notification requires defined semantics.","security_implication":"Subscribers must only receive events they are authorized to observe; event payloads should minimize sensitive data.","performance_implication":"Many observers increase notification fan-out cost; slow observers can affect producer latency if notifications are synchronous.","testing_guidance":"Test subscription, unsubscription, notification, observer failure, and ordering assumptions.","verification_guidance":"Verify documented delivery semantics rather than assuming stronger guarantees from the observer abstraction.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000020"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Explicit interface guidance applies to producer/observer contracts."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000020","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Observer","concept":"Observer Failure Isolation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What should happen if an observer fails while processing a notification?","answer":"The system must define whether one observer failure fails the producer operation, is isolated, is retried, or is recorded for later processing.","explanation":"Observer does not define failure semantics by itself. Synchronous and asynchronous notification have materially different operational behavior.","principle":"Delivery and failure semantics are part of the event contract.","rule":"Never assume observer failures are harmless or automatically isolated.","correct_example":"A synchronous observer contract explicitly states that notification failure aborts the operation, while an asynchronous implementation uses durable delivery and independent failure handling.","invalid_example":"Calling ten observers sequentially and silently ignoring all exceptions.","problem":"Producer behavior becomes unpredictable when observers fail.","failure_mode":"Important side effects are lost or producer state becomes inconsistent with observer state.","cause":"Notification semantics were unspecified.","solution":"Choose and document failure semantics appropriate to the business requirement.","alternative":"Durable event messaging for independently recoverable consumers.","tradeoff":"Failure isolation improves resilience but can introduce eventual consistency and operational complexity.","constraint":"Do not claim durable delivery from an in-process observer list.","edge_case":"An observer may partially process an event before failing; idempotent consumer handling may be required.","security_implication":"Failure logs and event payloads must avoid leaking sensitive information.","performance_implication":"Synchronous observers add their latency to the producer path; asynchronous delivery shifts cost but adds infrastructure or queueing complexity.","testing_guidance":"Inject observer failures and verify the documented producer and delivery outcome.","verification_guidance":"Verify that failure behavior matches the declared delivery contract.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000019","D06-PAT-000014"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ guidance emphasizes explicit contracts and failure signaling."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000021","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Template Method","concept":"Template Method Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a fixed algorithm skeleton allow controlled variation in selected steps?","answer":"Define the invariant algorithm sequence in one abstraction and delegate selected steps to overridable or injectable operations.","explanation":"Template Method keeps stable orchestration centralized while allowing controlled variation at explicit extension points.","principle":"Keep invariant sequencing centralized and isolate legitimate variation behind named hooks.","rule":"Only genuine variation points should be overridable or replaceable.","correct_example":"A data-processing workflow defines validate, transform, persist as a stable sequence while allowing format-specific transformation.","invalid_example":"A base class with dozens of overridable methods where subclasses can arbitrarily alter the entire algorithm.","problem":"Multiple implementations duplicate nearly identical workflows.","failure_mode":"Subclass overrides violate required sequencing or invariants.","cause":"Extension points are too broad or insufficiently constrained.","solution":"Minimize hooks and make the invariant algorithm difficult to bypass.","alternative":"Strategy when the entire algorithm varies rather than only selected steps.","tradeoff":"Strong control over workflow but may create inheritance coupling.","constraint":"Use only where the language's inheritance/composition model supports a clear contract.","edge_case":"Subclass behavior may require data unavailable at the hook; redesign the hook contract instead of reaching into hidden state.","security_implication":"Security-critical workflow steps must not be bypassable through subclass overrides.","performance_implication":"Virtual/dynamic dispatch overhead is normally secondary; benchmark only if the path is proven hot.","testing_guidance":"Test the fixed sequence and each legitimate variation independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify invariant steps always occur and cannot be skipped by supported extensions.","language":"Python; Java; C++; C#-like concepts not included in selected language set; TypeScript; Rust via traits/composition","version_range":"Pattern is language-independent; idiomatic implementation differs.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000001","D06-PAT-000022"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search8","relevance":"Python classes support inheritance and method overriding used by Template Method."},{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ interface guidance supports explicit contracts for extension points."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000022","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Template Method","concept":"Composition Over Template Inheritance","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should composition replace Template Method inheritance?","answer":"Prefer composition when behavior varies independently, when multiple combinations are needed, or when inheritance would create rigid coupling between the workflow and extension implementation.","explanation":"Template Method is useful for a stable algorithm skeleton, but composition can represent independent variation more directly.","principle":"Use inheritance for a stable conceptual subtype relationship, not merely for code reuse.","rule":"If variation dimensions multiply, separate them into composable strategies rather than creating subclasses for every combination.","correct_example":"A workflow composes a validator, transformer, and sink strategy rather than creating subclasses for every combination.","invalid_example":"Creating subclasses ValidatorAWithSinkB, ValidatorAWithSinkC, ValidatorBWithSinkB, and so on.","problem":"Subclass combinations grow combinatorially.","failure_mode":"Class hierarchy becomes rigid and difficult to extend.","cause":"Multiple independent variation axes were encoded as inheritance.","solution":"Extract variation axes into composable policies.","alternative":"Strategy or dependency injection.","tradeoff":"Composition increases explicit wiring but reduces hierarchy explosion.","constraint":"Composition should remain understandable; excessive micro-components can also become difficult to reason about.","edge_case":"A tightly cohesive algorithm with one small extension point may still be clearer as Template Method.","security_implication":"Explicit composition can make security boundaries more visible than deeply inherited behavior.","performance_implication":"Composition can add dispatch but generally improves structural flexibility; measure critical paths.","testing_guidance":"Test each variation independently and important combinations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each composition satisfies the workflow's invariant contract.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000001","D06-PAT-000021"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ Core Guidelines discuss interface quality and avoiding error-prone coupling."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000023","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Command","concept":"Command as Audit Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is a command representation useful for auditability?","answer":"A command can provide an explicit representation of an intended operation, its parameters, actor/context metadata, and execution outcome, making it suitable as an audit boundary when the surrounding system defines those semantics.","explanation":"The pattern can separate intent representation from execution, but auditability requires explicit metadata and trustworthy outcome recording; a Command object alone is not an audit system.","principle":"Represent intent explicitly when intent and execution need separate evidence.","rule":"Do not treat construction of a command as proof that execution occurred or succeeded.","correct_example":"An authorized operation request is represented by a command record, then execution produces a separate outcome record.","invalid_example":"Logging a command creation event and labeling it as successful execution.","problem":"Intent and execution evidence are conflated.","failure_mode":"Audit trail claims actions occurred when they were only requested.","cause":"No distinction between command submission and verified outcome.","solution":"Represent requested, accepted, executed, and verified states separately.","alternative":"Direct operation with structured audit events.","tradeoff":"Explicit command lifecycle improves auditability but increases state and record management.","constraint":"Outcome verification remains outside the Command pattern itself.","edge_case":"A command can be accepted but fail during execution; audit records must preserve both events.","security_implication":"Command records may contain sensitive intent or authorization information and require appropriate access controls.","performance_implication":"Audit persistence adds I/O; batching or asynchronous recording may change durability semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test lifecycle transitions from creation through rejection, execution failure, success, and verification.","verification_guidance":"Verify that audit state corresponds to observed execution evidence rather than intent alone.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000013","D06-PAT-000014"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Explicit contract guidance supports separating operation interfaces from observed outcomes."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000024","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Structural Patterns","subtopic":"Null Object","concept":"Null Object Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can a Null Object eliminate repeated absence checks safely?","answer":"Use a Null Object when the absence case has a well-defined neutral behavior that is semantically valid wherever the normal abstraction is expected.","explanation":"The pattern replaces repeated null/None checks with an explicit implementation of the same interface whose behavior represents absence.","principle":"Make a valid absence state explicit when it has meaningful neutral semantics.","rule":"Do not use Null Object to hide situations where absence is actually an error that callers must handle.","correct_example":"A NoOpMetricsSink implements the metrics interface and intentionally discards metrics when metrics collection is configured as optional.","invalid_example":"A NoOpAuthorizationService that returns success when authorization configuration is missing.","problem":"Repeated absence checks obscure business logic.","failure_mode":"A neutral-looking object silently suppresses an important failure.","cause":"Absence was incorrectly classified as semantically valid.","solution":"Use Null Object only for explicitly valid neutral behavior and fail for required dependencies.","alternative":"Option/Maybe/Optional/result-based absence representation.","tradeoff":"Simplifies callers but can hide configuration mistakes if the null behavior is not carefully defined.","constraint":"The null implementation must preserve the interface contract.","edge_case":"A method whose normal return value has no meaningful neutral equivalent should not be silently fabricated.","security_implication":"Never use a permissive Null Object for authentication, authorization, validation, or security policy.","performance_implication":"Null Object can reduce branching overhead but this should not be the primary justification.","testing_guidance":"Test that the null implementation has the intended neutral behavior and cannot accidentally become permissive.","verification_guidance":"Verify that absence is explicitly allowed by the requirement and that no security-critical guarantee is weakened.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000025"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Strong interface contracts are necessary for a Null Object to remain substitutable."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000025","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Structural Patterns","subtopic":"Null Object","concept":"Fail-Closed Absence Handling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"When must absence be treated as failure rather than as a neutral object?","answer":"Absence must be treated as failure whenever the missing dependency, policy, identity, capability, or validation result is required for a safe operation.","explanation":"A permissive absence representation can convert configuration or authorization failures into successful-looking behavior.","principle":"Security-critical absence should not default to permission or success.","rule":"Required security controls must fail closed when their required state is unavailable.","correct_example":"Missing authorization policy causes the operation to be denied rather than substituted with an allow-all Null Object.","invalid_example":"Returning a permissive default policy when the authorization service cannot be loaded.","problem":"Optionality is confused with security-critical absence.","failure_mode":"A missing dependency becomes an implicit bypass.","cause":"Neutral default was chosen without considering security semantics.","solution":"Classify dependencies as optional or mandatory and define explicit failure behavior.","alternative":"Explicit Result/Option representation with mandatory caller handling.","tradeoff":"Fail-closed behavior may reduce availability during configuration failures but protects required security properties.","constraint":"The security requirement must define what failure means and which operations are affected.","edge_case":"Emergency fallback behavior may exist, but it must itself be explicitly authorized and bounded.","security_implication":"Directly security-critical; permissive Null Objects can create authorization bypasses.","performance_implication":"Failure-path handling should remain observable without adding unnecessary latency to normal operation.","testing_guidance":"Inject missing policy/dependency conditions and verify denial or required safe behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every required security dependency has a tested failure path that cannot silently permit protected operations.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000024","D06-PAT-000026"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ Core Guidelines emphasize explicit interfaces, preconditions, and postconditions."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000026","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Structural Patterns","subtopic":"Dependency Inversion","concept":"Explicit Dependency Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should a component avoid hard-coding a concrete dependency when the dependency may vary?","answer":"Depend on a narrow, explicit contract and provide the concrete implementation through composition rather than constructing it inside the consumer.","explanation":"Explicit dependency boundaries make variation, testing, and replacement easier while reducing hidden coupling.","principle":"Consumers should depend on the behavior they require rather than on unnecessary construction details.","rule":"Inject only dependencies that the component actually uses; do not create an abstraction merely for theoretical replaceability.","correct_example":"A report service receives a formatter interface/callable rather than importing and constructing one concrete formatter internally.","invalid_example":"A service constructs database, logger, HTTP client, formatter, and configuration objects internally regardless of caller requirements.","problem":"Concrete construction prevents controlled substitution and complicates testing.","failure_mode":"Changes to one concrete dependency ripple through consumers.","cause":"Construction and use were coupled.","solution":"Move composition to an outer boundary and expose the narrow dependency contract.","alternative":"Direct construction when the dependency is intrinsic, stable, and cheap to control.","tradeoff":"Improves substitution and testing but requires explicit wiring.","constraint":"Abstractions should correspond to real variation or boundary requirements.","edge_case":"Over-injection can make APIs difficult to use and obscure which dependencies are truly required.","security_implication":"Dependency injection does not itself establish trust; injected implementations must still be authorized and trusted.","performance_implication":"Injected indirection is usually minor; construction reuse may have larger performance consequences and must respect lifecycle requirements.","testing_guidance":"Inject deterministic test doubles or real implementations as appropriate and verify the consumer's contract.","verification_guidance":"Verify the component's behavior against the dependency contract rather than its concrete implementation details.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000001","D06-PAT-000009"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Official C++ guidance emphasizes explicit, strongly typed interfaces and contracts."},{"source_ref":"turn0search8","relevance":"Python's class model supports composition and polymorphic objects used to implement explicit dependency boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000027","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Specification","concept":"Specification Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can reusable business predicates be composed without duplicating conditional logic?","answer":"Represent domain predicates as explicit specifications that can be combined using logical composition such as AND, OR, and NOT.","explanation":"Specification turns a business condition into a reusable object or callable, allowing complex predicates to be named, tested, and composed.","principle":"Make important business predicates explicit when they recur or require independent testing.","rule":"A specification should represent a coherent predicate rather than arbitrary workflow logic.","correct_example":"EligibleForDiscount combines IsActiveCustomer AND HasMinimumSpend.","invalid_example":"A specification object that performs database writes, sends notifications, and changes authorization state while evaluating a predicate.","problem":"Repeated business conditions drift across callers.","failure_mode":"Specifications become hidden services rather than pure or clearly bounded predicates.","cause":"Behavior unrelated to evaluation was included.","solution":"Keep evaluation focused and move side effects to separate operations.","alternative":"Direct predicate functions for simple one-off conditions.","tradeoff":"Improves reuse and composability but can become abstract if over-modeled.","constraint":"The meaning of the predicate must be stable and documented.","edge_case":"Null or incomplete data must have explicitly defined predicate semantics.","security_implication":"Security predicates must be evaluated against authoritative identity/authorization data and must not rely on client-provided assertions.","performance_implication":"Composed predicates may repeat expensive checks; short-circuiting and evaluation ordering can matter.","testing_guidance":"Test atomic specifications, compositions, boundary values, and contradictory predicates.","verification_guidance":"Verify predicate truth values against explicit acceptance examples and counterexamples.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000001","D06-PAT-000028"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search3","relevance":"Rust documentation supports treating closures/functions as composable behavior values."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000028","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Specification","concept":"Pure Specification","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should a reusable specification preferably avoid side effects?","answer":"A side-effect-free specification is easier to compose, test, cache where appropriate, reason about, and reuse in different execution contexts.","explanation":"When evaluating a predicate changes system state or performs external I/O, the predicate stops behaving like a simple condition and becomes an operation with hidden consequences.","principle":"Separate deciding whether a condition holds from performing the action that follows the decision.","rule":"A reusable specification should normally observe inputs and return a decision without mutating external state.","correct_example":"is_eligible(customer) returns a boolean or structured decision without changing customer state.","invalid_example":"is_eligible(customer) writes a database record as a side effect before returning true.","problem":"Predicate evaluation becomes unpredictable and difficult to test.","failure_mode":"Calling the same specification multiple times changes the system.","cause":"Decision and execution responsibilities were combined.","solution":"Extract side effects into explicit command/service operations.","alternative":"A policy evaluation service when evaluation necessarily depends on external authoritative state; make I/O explicit.","tradeoff":"Purity improves reasoning but may require passing more data into the predicate.","constraint":"Pure does not mean independent of all external data; it means evaluation does not mutate external state.","edge_case":"Time-dependent policies may require an explicit evaluation timestamp to make results reproducible.","security_implication":"Explicit inputs make policy dependencies easier to audit, but authoritative identity and policy data must remain protected.","performance_implication":"Pure predicates can sometimes be reused or memoized, but caching is valid only when inputs fully determine the result.","testing_guidance":"Use deterministic examples and counterexamples.","verification_guidance":"Verify repeated evaluation with identical inputs produces identical results unless the contract explicitly includes changing context.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000027","D06-PAT-000023"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search14","relevance":"Rust official documentation discusses functional-style use of functions as values and composable transformations."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000029","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Functional Patterns","subtopic":"Functional Core","concept":"Functional Core / Imperative Shell","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can complex business logic be made easier to test while keeping necessary I/O at the boundaries?","answer":"Keep deterministic transformations and decisions in a functional core while isolating I/O, mutation, and external effects in an imperative shell.","explanation":"The pattern separates reasoning-heavy logic from environmental interaction, allowing the core to be tested with explicit inputs and outputs.","principle":"Keep pure decision logic independent from external effects when practical.","rule":"The core should not secretly perform external I/O merely because it is conceptually part of a decision.","correct_example":"Input data enters a pure pricing calculation, which returns a decision; a separate shell persists the result.","invalid_example":"A pricing function reads configuration from global state, queries a database, sends a notification, and returns a price.","problem":"Business logic becomes difficult to test and reproduce.","failure_mode":"Tests depend on environment state and external systems.","cause":"Effects and decisions were mixed.","solution":"Move effects to the boundary and pass required data explicitly into the core.","alternative":"Layered application services or dependency-injected components.","tradeoff":"Improves deterministic testing and reasoning but may require explicit data flow and boundary code.","constraint":"Not every system can be completely pure; the goal is useful separation, not ideological purity.","edge_case":"External decisions such as current time should be passed as explicit context when reproducibility matters.","security_implication":"Boundary isolation can make trust transitions and validation points clearer.","performance_implication":"Pure transformations may be easier to benchmark independently; copying large data structures can offset benefits in some languages.","testing_guidance":"Test the functional core extensively with deterministic inputs and separately test the shell's integration behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the core's outputs depend only on declared inputs and context.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000027","D06-PAT-000028"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search14","relevance":"Rust official documentation discusses functional-style programming with functions, closures, and iterators."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000030","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Functional Patterns","subtopic":"Functional Core","concept":"Explicit Context Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should changing environmental values be handled when deterministic logic depends on them?","answer":"Represent relevant environmental values as explicit context inputs rather than reading hidden global state from the decision logic.","explanation":"Explicit context makes dependencies visible and allows deterministic replay of decisions when the context is preserved.","principle":"Make decision inputs explicit when reproducibility or verification matters.","rule":"Only include context values that actually influence the decision; avoid passing an opaque global context object everywhere.","correct_example":"calculate_due_date(order, current_date, holiday_calendar) rather than reading current date and global holiday configuration internally.","invalid_example":"A pure-looking function that silently reads system time, environment variables, and global configuration.","problem":"Results cannot be reliably reproduced from recorded inputs.","failure_mode":"Same input appears to produce different output without an observable reason.","cause":"Implicit environmental dependencies.","solution":"Inject the relevant context explicitly and define its version/validity semantics.","alternative":"Dependency object with explicit interfaces when many related context values are needed.","tradeoff":"Improves reproducibility but can increase parameter or context-management complexity.","constraint":"Context must be versioned or identified when its interpretation affects reproducibility.","edge_case":"Time zones, locale, feature flags, and policy versions can all materially change results.","security_implication":"Context must distinguish trusted system values from untrusted client-supplied values.","performance_implication":"Passing context is usually cheap; copying large context objects can be expensive and should be avoided where unnecessary.","testing_guidance":"Run the same decision with identical context and verify deterministic results.","verification_guidance":"Record the context required to reproduce important decisions.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000029","D06-PAT-000031"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ guidance emphasizes explicit interfaces and preconditions/postconditions."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000031","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Functional Patterns","subtopic":"Data Transformation","concept":"Parse Validate Transform","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should external structured data be handled before entering business logic?","answer":"Parse the external representation, validate its structural and semantic requirements, then transform it into an internal representation before business processing.","explanation":"Separating parsing, validation, and transformation prevents malformed external representations from being mistaken for trusted domain data.","principle":"Cross a trust or representation boundary through explicit validation and transformation.","rule":"Business logic should not silently assume that externally supplied representations satisfy internal invariants.","correct_example":"JSON request -> parse -> schema validation -> domain conversion -> business operation.","invalid_example":"Passing arbitrary decoded JSON objects directly into business logic and relying on later failures.","problem":"Representation errors and business errors become conflated.","failure_mode":"Malformed or unexpected data reaches components that assume stronger invariants.","cause":"Boundary validation was omitted or performed too late.","solution":"Define the boundary contract and validate before conversion to internal models.","alternative":"Typed deserialization that combines some parsing and validation, provided the resulting guarantees are understood.","tradeoff":"Adds boundary processing but improves clarity, diagnostics, and trust separation.","constraint":"Validation must correspond to actual domain invariants; syntax validation alone is insufficient.","edge_case":"Unknown fields, missing fields, duplicate fields, numeric limits, encoding, and versioned representations require explicit policy.","security_implication":"This pattern is directly relevant to input validation and reducing injection/data-confusion risks.","performance_implication":"Validation and transformation consume CPU/memory; optimize only after measuring real boundary costs.","testing_guidance":"Test malformed syntax, structurally valid but semantically invalid data, boundary values, and valid representations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that successful transformation establishes the documented internal invariants.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000032","D06-PAT-000033"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ Core Guidelines emphasize precise interfaces and preconditions."},{"source_ref":"turn0search7","relevance":"Java sealed types illustrate explicit modeling of permitted variants at type boundaries."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000032","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Functional Patterns","subtopic":"Data Transformation","concept":"Boundary Validation vs Domain Validation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Which validation belongs at a representation boundary and which belongs in the domain model?","answer":"Representation validation checks whether data can be safely interpreted; domain validation checks whether the interpreted data satisfies business invariants.","explanation":"Separating the two prevents transport-specific concerns from contaminating domain rules and prevents domain assumptions from being treated as mere syntax checks.","principle":"Validate according to the boundary and invariant being protected.","rule":"Do not treat schema validity as proof of domain validity.","correct_example":"A request schema verifies required fields and types, then a domain rule verifies that the requested transition is allowed.","invalid_example":"Accepting a structurally valid request as automatically authorized or semantically valid.","problem":"Different classes of validation are conflated.","failure_mode":"Valid-looking input bypasses business constraints.","cause":"Only representation-level validation was implemented.","solution":"Define separate structural, semantic, authorization, and domain validation layers where required.","alternative":"A single validated domain model if it genuinely establishes all required guarantees.","tradeoff":"Separation improves clarity but can produce repeated validation if boundaries are poorly designed.","constraint":"Avoid redundant checks that provide no additional guarantee.","edge_case":"The same field may have transport constraints and independent domain constraints.","security_implication":"Authorization must remain distinct from ordinary data validation.","performance_implication":"Repeated validation may cost CPU; preserve only checks that establish meaningful guarantees at each trust boundary.","testing_guidance":"Create separate tests for structural invalidity and domain invalidity.","verification_guidance":"Verify which invariant each validation step establishes.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000031","D06-PAT-000025"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ guidance explicitly distinguishes interface preconditions and postconditions."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000033","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Functional Patterns","subtopic":"Error Modeling","concept":"Result-Oriented Error Boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is explicit success-or-error data preferable to exceptions for a domain operation?","answer":"Explicit result/error values are useful when failure is an expected part of normal control flow and callers must deliberately handle the outcome.","explanation":"A result-oriented boundary makes success and failure explicit in the operation's data contract. It should not be treated as universally superior to exceptions.","principle":"Choose an error representation that matches whether failure is expected, exceptional, recoverable, and compositional.","rule":"Do not use a result value merely to avoid understanding the language's normal exception model; choose based on contract and control-flow needs.","correct_example":"A parser returns either a parsed value or a structured validation error that the caller is expected to handle.","invalid_example":"Returning a generic false value for every failure and discarding diagnostic information.","problem":"Expected failure paths are hidden or inconsistently handled.","failure_mode":"Callers ignore errors because the representation does not force meaningful handling.","cause":"Weak error contract.","solution":"Return structured error information with a clear success/failure contract.","alternative":"Language-native exceptions where failures are exceptional or naturally propagate through call stacks.","tradeoff":"Explicit results improve visible control flow but can create propagation boilerplate.","constraint":"The chosen error model must preserve diagnostic context and expected handling semantics.","edge_case":"A result may contain both a value and warning/partial-success information; model this explicitly rather than overloading success.","security_implication":"Error values should avoid exposing secrets, internal paths, credentials, or sensitive diagnostics to untrusted callers.","performance_implication":"Error representation overhead depends on language and workload; do not generalize performance from one language to another.","testing_guidance":"Test success, expected failure, malformed failure, and propagation behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify every expected failure has a defined handling path.","language":"Go; Rust; Python; Java; JavaScript; TypeScript; C++; C","version_range":"Language-specific error mechanisms differ.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000034"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search4","relevance":"Effective Go documents errors as values and explicit error returns."},{"source_ref":"turn0search18","relevance":"Official Go FAQ discusses explicit error handling and error interface behavior."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000034","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Functional Patterns","subtopic":"Error Modeling","concept":"Structured Error Context","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should an error retain enough context for diagnosis without exposing unnecessary sensitive information?","answer":"Represent errors with structured categories and relevant causal/context information while controlling which details cross trust boundaries.","explanation":"A useful error contract supports diagnosis and recovery without forcing callers to parse unstable human-readable messages.","principle":"Separate machine-actionable error identity from human-readable diagnostic detail.","rule":"Error messages should not be the sole machine-readable contract.","correct_example":"A structured error contains category, stable code, operation context, safe message, and optional internal cause metadata.","invalid_example":"Callers branch on exact English error strings and internal stack traces are returned directly to external clients.","problem":"Error handling becomes brittle and may expose sensitive implementation details.","failure_mode":"Minor message changes break callers or leak internal information.","cause":"No stable error contract or trust-boundary filtering.","solution":"Define structured error categories and separate internal diagnostics from externally safe representations.","alternative":"Language-native exception hierarchy with stable exception types/codes.","tradeoff":"More structured error modeling improves interoperability but increases schema maintenance.","constraint":"Stable error identifiers must remain compatible or versioned appropriately.","edge_case":"Nested causes may form long chains; preserve useful causal information without uncontrolled recursion or sensitive data leakage.","security_implication":"External error responses must be sanitized according to the trust boundary.","performance_implication":"Capturing deep diagnostic context can increase memory and logging cost.","testing_guidance":"Test error classification, cause preservation, safe external rendering, and malformed error states.","verification_guidance":"Verify that callers can handle errors using stable structured fields without parsing presentation text.","language":"Go; Python; Java; JavaScript; TypeScript; Rust; C++; C","version_range":"Language-specific implementation.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000033","D06-PAT-000035"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search4","relevance":"Effective Go documents returning detailed error information through the error interface."},{"source_ref":"turn0search18","relevance":"Official Go documentation discusses error values and diagnostic behavior."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000035","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Policy Composition","concept":"Policy Object","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a variable business or technical policy be represented independently of the operation applying it?","answer":"Represent the policy as an explicit object, callable, or interface whose contract defines the decision it makes.","explanation":"A policy object is a domain-oriented form of Strategy that names a decision rule rather than merely an algorithm implementation.","principle":"Make important variable decisions explicit and independently testable.","rule":"A policy should expose a decision contract and avoid performing unrelated side effects.","correct_example":"RetryPolicy determines whether an operation may be retried based on error category and attempt count.","invalid_example":"RetryPolicy also performs the retry, logs globally, mutates configuration, and sends alerts.","problem":"Decision rules are duplicated across operations.","failure_mode":"Policy changes require modifying many unrelated callers.","cause":"Policy was embedded inside orchestration logic.","solution":"Extract the decision into a focused policy contract and keep execution separate.","alternative":"Direct conditional logic for simple, stable policies.","tradeoff":"Improves reuse and testability but can create excessive policy objects for trivial conditions.","constraint":"The policy contract must identify all decision inputs that materially affect the result.","edge_case":"Policies may vary by version or tenant; those dimensions must be explicit rather than hidden.","security_implication":"Security policies must consume authoritative context and must not silently default to permissive behavior.","performance_implication":"Policy evaluation should remain lightweight when used on hot paths; expensive external policy lookups require explicit caching/lifecycle semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test policy decisions independently using positive, negative, and boundary cases.","verification_guidance":"Verify policy outputs against documented decision tables or acceptance criteria.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000001","D06-PAT-000027"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search3","relevance":"Rust official documentation supports functions and closures as explicit behavior values."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000036","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Structural Patterns","subtopic":"Facade","concept":"Facade Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should a complex subsystem be exposed through a simpler interface?","answer":"Use a Facade when callers repeatedly need a coherent subset of subsystem operations and a simpler boundary improves usability without hiding necessary semantics.","explanation":"A Facade provides a focused entry point over multiple components while preserving the subsystem's internal structure.","principle":"Expose the smallest coherent interface needed by a client.","rule":"A Facade should simplify interaction, not erase distinctions that callers must understand for correctness.","correct_example":"A document export facade coordinates validation, transformation, and output components behind export(document, format).","invalid_example":"A facade exposing one generic do_everything method that hides every failure and configuration detail.","problem":"Consumers must coordinate too many subsystem components.","failure_mode":"The facade becomes a god object or hides critical semantics.","cause":"The facade boundary was defined around convenience rather than a coherent use case.","solution":"Define a focused facade around a real client workflow and preserve important error semantics.","alternative":"Direct subsystem interfaces when clients genuinely need fine-grained control.","tradeoff":"Reduces coupling and usage complexity but may constrain advanced consumers.","constraint":"The facade must remain cohesive and should not become the new subsystem monolith.","edge_case":"Some clients may need operations intentionally excluded from the facade; provide lower-level access where appropriate rather than expanding the facade indefinitely.","security_implication":"A facade is a useful place to enforce a coherent authorization boundary, but authorization must match the actual operations exposed.","performance_implication":"Coordination may add minimal overhead; the main cost is usually the underlying subsystem operations.","testing_guidance":"Test facade workflows plus important error translations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the facade's documented workflow produces the required subsystem outcomes.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000009","D06-PAT-000026"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"Official C++ guidance emphasizes explicit interfaces and avoiding error-prone APIs."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000037","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Structural Patterns","subtopic":"Facade","concept":"Facade Scope Control","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What indicates that a Facade has become too broad?","answer":"A facade is too broad when unrelated workflows, policies, lifecycle responsibilities, and subsystem details accumulate behind one interface.","explanation":"A facade should simplify a coherent client interaction. A broad facade merely relocates complexity and creates another coupling hotspot.","principle":"A facade should reduce conceptual complexity, not merely hide it.","rule":"Split a facade when its methods represent unrelated responsibilities or require incompatible dependencies.","correct_example":"Separate ReportingFacade and ExportFacade when their workflows have independent responsibilities and dependencies.","invalid_example":"ApplicationFacade containing authentication, billing, reporting, file storage, notifications, deployment, and unrelated administrative operations.","problem":"Consumers depend on a large unrelated interface.","failure_mode":"Small changes cause widespread recompilation, testing, and deployment impact.","cause":"Convenience was prioritized over cohesion.","solution":"Split by coherent client workflows or domain boundaries.","alternative":"Multiple focused services/interfaces.","tradeoff":"More interfaces but lower coupling and clearer ownership.","constraint":"Do not split merely to maximize interface count; the boundary must improve reasoning or change isolation.","edge_case":"A single facade may legitimately coordinate several components when they form one coherent user-level operation.","security_implication":"Broad facades can accidentally expose privileged operations through a common boundary.","performance_implication":"Broad facades may initialize or depend on unnecessary subsystems; focused facades can reduce resource coupling.","testing_guidance":"Measure responsibility cohesion and test dependency impact when modifying facade methods.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each facade operation belongs to the same coherent responsibility boundary.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000036"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ interface guidance emphasizes interfaces that are understandable, efficient, and not error-prone."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000038","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Visitor","concept":"Visitor Pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can Visitor separate operations from a stable hierarchy of elements?","answer":"Visitor can be useful when the element type hierarchy is relatively stable but many independent operations must be performed across its variants.","explanation":"Visitor moves operations into visitor implementations, reducing the need to add every operation directly to each element type.","principle":"Separate a stable data/type structure from a growing set of operations when the language and domain make double dispatch appropriate.","rule":"Visitor is most useful when element variants are stable and operations vary more frequently than variants.","correct_example":"A compiler-like AST has stable node variants and separate visitors for validation, formatting, and analysis.","invalid_example":"Using Visitor when both node types and operations change frequently, creating frequent updates across visitors.","problem":"Many operations would otherwise be duplicated across a stable hierarchy.","failure_mode":"Adding a new element variant requires updating every visitor.","cause":"The tradeoff between stable variants and variable operations was ignored.","solution":"Use Visitor only when the change pattern favors stable elements and variable operations.","alternative":"Pattern matching or explicit dispatch where the language provides strong closed-variant support.","tradeoff":"Makes new operations easier but makes new element types more expensive to introduce.","constraint":"The hierarchy must have a meaningful stable abstraction.","edge_case":"A new variant may break exhaustiveness across every visitor implementation.","security_implication":"Visitor implementations may traverse sensitive structures; access control and data minimization still apply.","performance_implication":"Dynamic dispatch and traversal overhead depend on implementation; avoid assuming Visitor is faster or slower without measurement.","testing_guidance":"Test every visitor against every supported element variant and explicitly handle unsupported combinations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each visitor provides defined behavior for all permitted element types.","language":"Java; C++; Rust; Python; TypeScript; Go","version_range":"Language-dependent; closed pattern matching may be preferable in languages with strong algebraic data types.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000018","D06-PAT-000039"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search0","relevance":"Rust enums and pattern matching provide an alternative for closed variant operations."},{"source_ref":"turn0search7","relevance":"Java sealed hierarchies support closed type sets that can be processed systematically."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_PATTERN","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000039","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Visitor","concept":"Visitor Change-Direction Test","knowledge_type":"DECISION_RULE","question":"How should an engineer decide between Visitor and adding behavior to the element types?","answer":"Determine which dimension changes more frequently: element variants or operations. Visitor favors stable variants with frequently changing operations.","explanation":"The main tradeoff is the direction of change. Visitor is not universally better than direct methods or pattern matching.","principle":"Choose abstractions according to the dominant axis of change.","rule":"Do not select Visitor merely because it is a named design pattern; evaluate actual change frequency and ownership.","correct_example":"A stable AST receives many analysis passes owned by separate teams; visitor-like separation can localize those operations.","invalid_example":"A rapidly evolving event hierarchy is processed by dozens of visitors that all need modification whenever a new event is added.","problem":"Pattern selection increases change cost instead of reducing it.","failure_mode":"Every structural change causes widespread visitor updates.","cause":"The dominant change axis was misidentified.","solution":"Choose direct methods, pattern matching, or another dispatch mechanism when variants change frequently.","alternative":"Pattern matching, strategy dispatch, or direct polymorphic methods.","tradeoff":"Visitor optimizes one change dimension at the expense of the other.","constraint":"Ownership and release coordination also affect the practical cost of each choice.","edge_case":"Different subsystems may have different dominant change axes; one global choice may not fit every boundary.","security_implication":"Separate visitor implementations require the same authorization and data-access review as any other operation.","performance_implication":"Traversal may be shared or duplicated depending on design; benchmark actual workloads.","testing_guidance":"Model expected future changes and test the resulting modification surface.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the selected design minimizes the documented high-frequency change path.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-independent decision rule.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000038","D06-PAT-000022"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search10","relevance":"Rust official documentation explicitly compares alternative representations for state behavior, illustrating the importance of design trade-offs."}],"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"ENGINEERING_HEURISTIC","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

{"record_id":"D06-PAT-000040","dataset_id":"D06","topic":"Behavioral Patterns","subtopic":"Dispatch","concept":"Polymorphism vs Explicit Dispatch","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should polymorphic dispatch be preferred over explicit matching or conditional dispatch?","answer":"Prefer polymorphic dispatch when behavior varies with independently replaceable implementations; prefer explicit matching when the variant set is closed and centralized exhaustive reasoning is more valuable.","explanation":"Neither mechanism is universally superior. The choice depends on whether the domain is open or closed, where variation lives, and how changes are expected to occur.","principle":"Align the dispatch mechanism with the domain's extension model.","rule":"Use open polymorphism for genuine extensibility and closed dispatch for bounded variants where exhaustive handling matters.","correct_example":"A plugin-like extension point uses an interface/trait, while a fixed set of protocol message variants uses explicit variant matching.","invalid_example":"Using dynamic polymorphism for a tiny closed set solely because it is a familiar design pattern.","problem":"The dispatch mechanism does not match domain structure.","failure_mode":"Either extension becomes unnecessarily difficult or exhaustive correctness becomes difficult.","cause":"No explicit analysis of openness, closure, and change frequency.","solution":"Classify the variant set and expected change direction before selecting dispatch.","alternative":"Function strategy, sealed hierarchy, enum/tagged union, or direct conditional depending on language and domain.","tradeoff":"Open polymorphism favors extension; closed dispatch favors exhaustiveness and locality.","constraint":"Language capabilities differ; do not transfer one language's idiom directly to another.","edge_case":"A currently closed domain may become extensible later; version the decision rather than prematurely designing for unlimited extension.","security_implication":"Closed dispatch can constrain unexpected implementations; open extension points require stronger extension trust controls.","performance_implication":"Dispatch costs vary by language/runtime/compiler; benchmark if dispatch is a measured hotspot.","testing_guidance":"Test all known variants and extension implementations appropriate to the selected model.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the chosen model makes the intended completeness or extensibility property observable.","language":"Python; JavaScript; TypeScript; Java; C; C++; Rust; Go","version_range":"Language-specific.","related_records":["D06-PAT-000001","D06-PAT-000016","D06-PAT-000018"],"provenance":[{"source_ref":"turn0search0","relevance":"Rust official documentation describes explicit enum variants and matching."},{"source_ref":"turn0search7","relevance":"Oracle documents sealed classes/interfaces for controlled subtype hierarchies."},{"source_ref":"turn0search5","relevance":"C++ Core Guidelines emphasize explicit and strongly typed interfaces."}],"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"DOCUMENTATION_SUPPORTED","created_at":"2026-09-16","updated_at":"2026-09-16"}

