{"record_id":"D08-B88-R03","dataset_id":"D08","batch_id":"B88","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","topic":"Performance Testing","example_title":"Memory Measurement With Tracemalloc","code":"import tracemalloc\n\ndef build_values(count: int) -> list[int]:\n    return list(range(count))\n\ndef measure_peak(count: int) -> int:\n    tracemalloc.start()\n    try:\n        build_values(count)\n        _, peak = tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()\n        return peak\n    finally:\n        tracemalloc.stop()\n\npeak = measure_peak(1000)\nassert peak >= 0\n","expected_behavior":"The example records the peak memory tracked by tracemalloc during the operation.","why_valid":"tracemalloc provides runtime tracing of Python memory allocations within its tracing scope.","preconditions":["Python 3.11+","tracemalloc available"],"edge_cases":["Native allocations not tracked by tracemalloc","Tracing overhead","Concurrent allocations"],"security_implications":"Memory measurement can help identify unexpected allocation growth, but does not prove absence of memory vulnerabilities.","performance_characteristics":"Tracing adds runtime and memory overhead; measured values are environment-dependent.","testing_method":"Compare measurements across controlled workloads and ensure tracing overhead is considered.","verification_method":"Execution required for actual measurement; no measured result is claimed.","version_notes":"Uses standard-library tracemalloc APIs.","relationships":["D08-B05-R01","D08-B88-R01","D08-B89-R01"],"provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Python documentation","title":"Python 3.11 Documentation","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3.11/library/tracemalloc.html","version":"3.11","section":"tracemalloc","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevance":"Direct memory-tracing API semantics."},"verification_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
{"record_id":"D08-B88-R04","dataset_id":"D08","batch_id":"B88","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","topic":"Performance Testing","example_title":"Profile-Guided Function Measurement","code":"import cProfile\nimport pstats\nimport io\n\ndef workload() -> int:\n    return sum(i * i for i in range(1000))\n\ndef profile_workload() -> str:\n    profiler = cProfile.Profile()\n    profiler.enable()\n    try:\n        workload()\n    finally:\n        profiler.disable()\n    output = io.StringIO()\n    pstats.Stats(profiler, stream=output).sort_stats(\"cumulative\").print_stats(10)\n    return output.getvalue()\n\nreport = profile_workload()\nassert isinstance(report, str)\n","expected_behavior":"The example collects a cProfile report for the workload and returns the formatted report text.","why_valid":"Profiling is used to gather execution evidence before optimization rather than assuming the bottleneck.","preconditions":["Python 3.11+","cProfile and pstats available"],"edge_cases":["Very small workload","Profiling overhead","I/O-heavy workloads"],"security_implications":"Profiling output can contain sensitive function arguments or paths depending on the workload; reports should be handled accordingly.","performance_characteristics":"Profiling introduces measurement overhead; report generation also consumes resources.","testing_method":"Run the profiler and inspect the resulting call statistics.","verification_method":"Execution required for actual profile data; no bottleneck conclusion is claimed.","version_notes":"Uses cProfile, pstats, and io standard-library APIs.","relationships":["D08-B70-R03","D08-B88-R01","D08-B88-R05"],"provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Python documentation","title":"Python 3.11 Documentation","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3.11/library/profile.html","version":"3.11","section":"cProfile and pstats","retrieval_date":"2026-09-16","relevan