{"record_id":"D08-B90-R01","dataset_id":"D08","batch_id":"B90","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","topic":"Integrated Verification","example_title":"Validate Then Audit Then Execute","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Command:\n    actor: str\n    action: str\n\nclass Audit:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.records: list[str] = []\n    def record(self, message: str) -> None:\n        self.records.append(message)\n\ndef execute(command: Command, audit: Audit) -> str:\n    if command.actor != \"operator\":\n        raise PermissionError(\"unauthorized\")\n    audit.record(f\"authorized:{command.action}\")\n    return f\"executed:{command.action}\"\n\ndef test_integrated_command() -> None:\n    audit = Audit()\n    result = execute(Command(\"operator\", \"restart\"), audit)\n    assert result == \"executed:restart\"\n    assert audit.records == [\"authorized:restart\"]\n","expected_behavior":"Authorization is evaluated before the side effect result is returned, and the authorization event is recorded.","why_valid":"The example keeps policy evaluation, audit recording, and command execution explicit and testable.","preconditions":["Python 3.11+","The actor and action contract is intentionally simplified"],"edge_cases":["Unauthorized actor","Audit failure","Duplicate execution","Unknown action"],"security_implications":"Real systems need authenticated identity, scoped authorization, durable audit guarantees, and explicit action verification; this example does not provide those by itself.","performance_characteristics":"O(1) for the demonstrated in-memory policy and audit operations.","testing_method":"Test authorized and unauthorized commands and audit behavior.","verification_method":"Runtime execution required; not performed during dataset generation.","version_notes":"Uses dataclass and list semantics available in Python 3.11+.","relationships":["D08-B85-R02","D08-B87-R