{"record_id":"D08-B92-R01","dataset_id":"D08","batch_id":"B92","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","topic":"Concurrency Testing","example_title":"Thread-Safe Counter Test","code":"from threading import Lock, Thread\n\nclass SafeCounter:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.value = 0\n        self._lock = Lock()\n\n    def increment(self) -> None:\n        with self._lock:\n            self.value += 1\n\ndef test_concurrent_increment() -> None:\n    counter = SafeCounter()\n    threads = [Thread(target=lambda: [counter.increment() for _ in range(100)]) for _ in range(4)]\n    for thread in threads:\n        thread.start()\n    for thread in threads:\n        thread.join()\n    assert counter.value == 400\n","expected_behavior":"Concurrent increments are protected by a lock and the final value equals the total number of completed increments.","why_valid":"The shared mutation is enclosed within one synchronization boundary.","preconditions":["Python 3.11+","Thread scheduling completes successfully"],"edge_cases":["Thread failure","Deadlock","Lock contention","Process termination during update"],"security_implications":"Synchronization is important when shared state influences authorization, accounting, quotas, or security decisions.","performance_characteristics":"Lock contention increases with concurrent writers; no benchmark is claimed.","testing_method":"Run multiple threads and verify final state.","verification_method":"Runtime execution under repeated scheduling conditions; not executed during dataset generation.","version_notes":"Uses threading.Lock and Thread.","relationships":["D08-B18-R01","D08-B31-R01","D08-B92-R02"],"provenance":{"source_type":"official_documentation","source":"Python documentation","title":"Python 3.11 Documentation","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","url":"https://docs.python.org/3.11/library/threading.html","version":"3.11","section":"Lock and Thread","retrieval_date":"2026-09-