{"record_id":"D09-B100-R01","dataset_id":"D09","batch_id":"B100","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","topic":"transactional boundary","example_title":"Assuming a database rollback reverses an external side effect","invalid_code":"def transfer(db, payment_gateway, amount):\n    payment_gateway.charge(amount)\n    try:\n        db.execute('INSERT INTO payments(amount) VALUES (?)', (amount,))\n        db.commit()\n    except Exception:\n        db.rollback()\n        raise","failure_type":"Distributed transaction contract failure","expected_error":"No Python exception is required for the conceptual defect; a database rollback cannot automatically undo a previously completed external gateway charge.","why_it_fails":"The database transaction and external side effect have independent durability and rollback semantics.","diagnostic_evidence":"payment_gateway.charge() executes before the database transaction completes.","likely_cause":"Treating external effects as if they participate in the local database transaction.","corrected_code":"def transfer(db, payment_gateway, amount, operation_id):\n    db.execute('INSERT INTO payments(operation_id, amount, status) VALUES (?, ?, ?)', (operation_id, amount, 'pending'))\n    db.commit()\n    try:\n        payment_gateway.charge(amount, idempotency_key=operation_id)\n    except Exception:\n        db.execute('UPDATE payments SET status = ? WHERE operation_id = ?', ('failed', operation_id))\n        db.commit()\n        raise\n    db.execute('UPDATE payments SET status = ? WHERE operation_id = ?', ('completed', operation_id))\n    db.commit()","expected_correct_behavior":"External side effects have explicit state, idempotency, recovery, and verification semantics rather than relying on a local rollback.","preconditions":"External service supports an appropriate idempotency mechanism or equivalent reconciliation strategy.","edge_cases":"The external charge may succeed while the local status 