{"record_id":"D09-B100-R03","dataset_id":"D09","batch_id":"B100","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","topic":"remote-operation verification","example_title":"Treating local request completion as proof of remote success","invalid_code":"def provision(client, resource_id):\n    response = client.create(resource_id)\n    if response.status_code == 202:\n        return True\n    return False","failure_type":"Asynchronous-operation verification failure","expected_error":"No Python exception is required; an accepted asynchronous request does not necessarily prove that the remote resource was successfully created.","why_it_fails":"A response indicating acceptance can represent queued work rather than completed state.","diagnostic_evidence":"The code converts an acceptance response directly into a completed-success boolean.","likely_cause":"Conflating request acceptance with operation completion.","corrected_code":"def provision(client, resource_id):\n    response = client.create(resource_id)\n    if response.status_code not in {200, 201, 202}:\n        return False\n    operation_id = response.operation_id\n    if response.status_code == 202:\n        result = client.wait_for_completion(operation_id)\n        return result.status == 'completed'\n    return True","expected_correct_behavior":"The implementation distinguishes request acceptance from verified remote completion.","preconditions":"The remote API exposes an operation status or equivalent completion signal.","edge_cases":"Timeouts and ambiguous remote states require reconciliation rather than automatic failure or success.","security_implications":"Acting on unverified provisioning state can grant access or expose resources that were not actually configured as intended.","performance_characteristics":"Polling or completion checks add latency and API calls.","testing_method":"Use a controlled asynchronous API that returns accepted-before-completion states.","verification_m