{"record_id":"D09-B100-R04","dataset_id":"D09","batch_id":"B100","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","topic":"graceful shutdown","example_title":"Cancelling a worker before durable checkpoint","invalid_code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def worker(state):\n    state['processed'] += 1\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    state['checkpoint'] = state['processed']\n\nasync def shutdown(task):\n    task.cancel()\n    await task\n\nasync def main():\n    state = {'processed': 0, 'checkpoint': 0}\n    task = asyncio.create_task(worker(state))\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.1)\n    try:\n        await shutdown(task)\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        pass\n    print(state)\n\nasyncio.run(main())","failure_type":"Shutdown consistency failure","expected_error":"No exception is required beyond cancellation; state can indicate work was started without a durable checkpoint proving recovery position.","why_it_fails":"The task is cancelled while awaiting before the checkpoint is recorded, leaving in-memory state inconsistent with durable progress.","diagnostic_evidence":"processed is incremented before the cancellation point while checkpoint is written afterward.","likely_cause":"Cancellation policy ignores the boundary between volatile work and durable state.","corrected_code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def worker(state):\n    state['processed'] += 1\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        state['checkpoint'] = state['processed']\n        raise\n    state['checkpoint'] = state['processed']\n\nasync def main():\n    state = {'processed': 0, 'checkpoint': 0}\n    task = asyncio.create_task(worker(state))\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.1)\n    task.cancel()\n    try:\n        await task\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        pass\n    print(state)\n\nasyncio.run(main())","expected_correct_behavior":"Cancellation establishes an explicit recovery/checkpoint boundary before the task terminates, while preserving cancellation semantics.","preconditions":"Checkpoint operation itself is cancellation-safe or otherwise durably coordinated.","edge_cases":"A real durable checkpoint may require a database or journal and cannot be represented by in-memory state alone.","security_implications":"Incomplete shutdown can leave security state, audit state, or containment progress inconsistent.","performance_characteristics":"Durable checkpoints add I/O and synchronization cost.","testing_method":"Inject cancellation at every defined cancellation point and inspect recovery state.","verification_method":"Confirm each interrupted operation has a defined recovery state and cancellation remains observable.","version_notes":"asyncio cancellation semantics apply to Python 3.11+. Durable checkpoint design is application-specific.","relationships":{"contrasts_with_dataset":"D08","feeds_debugging":"D10","feeds_root_cause":"D36","feeds_fix":"D37","feeds_architecture":"D41"},"provenance":{"source":"https://docs.python.org/3.11/library/asyncio-task.html#task-cancellation","source_type":"official_documentation","title":"Coroutines and Tasks — Task Cancellation","publisher":"Python Software Foundation","version":"3.11","section":"Task cancellation","retrieval_date":null,"provenance_note":"Python documentation supports cancellation semantics; durable checkpoint behavior is an application-level design requirement."},"verification_status":"NOT_EXECUTED"}
